{
  "requirement": "The harness instructs the model to finish by calling\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.submission.mcp`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.submission` has one obligation. The harness must instruct the model to finish by calling `submit_result`. The tool's shape and acceptance behavior belong to the cited `submission.mcp` and `submission.acceptance`, so I did not reassess them here.\n\nTests that assert the instruction text reaches the model:\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` runs four requests against a local server over two independent calls. For every captured request body it asserts that the serialized input contains \"Finish by calling submit_result\". It also checks that `submit_result` is offered as a tool.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat]` captures both native chat requests. It asserts that the serialized `messages` contain \"Finish by calling submit_result\" on each turn. The mocked model then actually calls `submit_result` and the answer is accepted.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` asserts that the first request's chat history contains \"Finish by calling submit_result\". It also checks the reminder text that follows when the model replies with plain text.\n\nOther marked tests are `default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix` and `typed_submission_references_resolve_and_invalid_results_are_agent_errors`. They cover the `submit_result` tool and its schema. They do not assert the instruction text, but the tests above already do.\n\nBecause the assertions check the instruction text itself in the outgoing requests, a harness that omitted or changed the instruction would fail them. They cover both the OpenAI Responses and Ollama backends, and the model-facing default-agent path. I found no untested obligation and no gap, and there are no acknowledgments to account for.",
